  Method: NO PING IS USED ANYWHERE IN THIS CASE. A ping would succeed identically with
  proxy ARP on or off, because the DUT routes between vlan10 and vlan48 either way, and
  would therefore prove nothing about the feature. Every leg is a crafted ARP request
  and the measurement is who answered and with which MAC. The four targets are chosen so
  that one leg answers four questions at once:
    192.168.48.5    routed out vlan48, a DIFFERENT interface from the ingress one
                    => the proxy-ARP case. MUST be answered when the feature is on.
    10.99.99.99     no route at all => MUST NOT be answered, on or off.
    192.168.10.78   inside vlan10's own subnet, the SAME interface the request arrives
                    on => MUST NOT be answered by proxy ARP.
    192.168.10.1    the DUT's OWN address on the receiving interface => the INSTRUMENT
                    CONTROL. MUST be answered in every leg; without it, "no reply"
                    cannot be told apart from "the instrument is not working".

STEP 0 -- baseline (feature OFF): prove the default and prove the instrument  done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The CLI surface first, read-only, in interface mode, with no command executed:

awplus(config-if)# ip proxy-arp ?
  <cr>
awplus(config-if)# ip local-proxy-arp ?
  <cr>
                                         <-- neither takes a sub-option

awplus# show running-config | include proxy
awplus#                                  <-- no proxy line: OFF by default
awplus# clear arp-cache

  leg `L0-proxy-off`:
  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L0-proxy-off --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth3 --arp 192.168.48.5,10.99.99.99,192.168.10.78,192.168.10.1 \
    --sender-ip 192.168.10.2 --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 --count 3 --wait 5

LEG L0-proxy-off  sent total: 12 frame(s)
  cap L0-proxy-off-eth3.pcap: 15 frame(s)
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.5  tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.99.99.99   tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.10.78 tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.10.1  tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43     [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
