  cap L1-proxy-on-eth3.pcap: 18 frame(s)
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.5  tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.5 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43     [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.99.99.99   tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.10.78 tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.10.1  tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43     [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]

  STEP 0 against STEP 2, identical traffic, one configuration line between them:

    target          nature                              OFF        ON
    192.168.48.5    routed out a DIFFERENT interface    0 replies  3 replies, is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
    10.99.99.99     no route                            0 replies  0 replies
    192.168.10.78   same subnet as the ingress iface    0 replies  0 replies
    192.168.10.1    the DUT's own address (control)     3 replies  3 replies

  => STEP 2 PASS: the DUT answers for 192.168.48.5 with ITS OWN MAC, e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
     -- the host is told "send it to me" for an address that is not the DUT's, which is
     proxy ARP. It does NOT answer for an address it has no route to, so the feature is
     gated on reachability rather than applied blindly; and it does NOT answer for an
     address on the receiving interface's own subnet even with the feature on, which is
     the boundary of `ip proxy-arp`, measured rather than assumed. The control is
     unchanged across the two legs, so nothing about the instrument moved between them.

STEP 3 -- the feature is per-interface, proven in both directions           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: `ip proxy-arp` is on vlan10 only. The mirror-image question is asked from the
  other wire -- an ARP request arriving on eth4 (vlan48) for 192.168.10.5, which the DUT
  can reach out vlan10. Structurally identical to STEP 2, but on an interface without
  the command.

  3a. With proxy ARP on vlan10 ONLY:
  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L2-other-side --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth4 --arp 192.168.10.5,192.168.48.1 --sender-ip 192.168.48.2 \
    --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:02 --count 3 --wait 5
cap L2-other-side-eth4.pcap: 9 frame(s)
  x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.10.5 tell 192.168.48.2  [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.1 tell 192.168.48.2  [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x3  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43    [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:02]
                                         <-- 192.168.10.5: 0 replies. Control
                                             192.168.48.1: 3 -- the instrument is alive
                                             on this wire too

  3b. Add the command to vlan48 and ask again, changing NOTHING else:
awplus(config)# interface vlan48
awplus(config-if)# ip proxy-arp
awplus# show running-config | include proxy
 ip proxy-arp
 ip proxy-arp                            <-- now two lines, one per interface

      leg `L4-both-sides`, the same invocation as 3a:
cap L4-both-sides-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)
  x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.10.5 tell 192.168.48.2  [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x3  ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.5 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43    [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:02]
  x3  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.1 tell 192.168.48.2  [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x3  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43    [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:02]

  => STEP 3 PASS: the vlan48 side went from 0 replies to 3 for exactly the same request,
     with one configuration line as the only change. So 3a's silence was the
     CONFIGURATION and not something about vlan48, the wire or the instrument:
     `ip proxy-arp` is per-interface, and the DUT applies it on the interface the
     request ARRIVES on.

STEP 4 -- the proxied address is genuinely reachable through the DUT        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a host that believed STEP 2's reply would now send its traffic for
  192.168.48.5 to the DUT's MAC. Do exactly that, and watch the far side.

  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L3-endtoend --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth3 --watch eth4 --probe 192.168.48.5 --sender-ip 192.168.10.2 \
    --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 5 \
    --answer 192.168.48.5=00:11:22:33:44:05 --answer-iface eth4 --wait 6

cap L3-endtoend-eth3.pcap:
  x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.5 :9 [ours]  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
cap L3-endtoend-eth4.pcap: 7 frame(s)
  x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.5 :9 [ours]  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:05]
  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.5 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.5 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:05    [00:11:22:33:44:05 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]

  => STEP 4 PASS: all 5 frames sent to the DUT's MAC on the vlan10 side came out on the
     vlan48 side addressed to the real owner's MAC, the DUT having resolved 192.168.48.5
     on vlan48 itself first (one request, one reply from the responder). The proxy-ARP
     answer therefore leads to real delivery, not just to a reply -- the mechanism is
     complete end to end.

